// Lesson 3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4). This is the lesson the course has been
// building toward: everything a projectile needs has already been done once,
// somewhere else, wearing different clothes. So nothing here is given — the
// student splits the problem, looks each part up, and writes the script.
//
// GROUND TRUTH: Projectile.cs is an empty stub but IS already attached to
// Turret_Projectile.prefab. That prefab has NO Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D,
// so any physics-based movement option costs the student an added component.
// Turret.prefab has a child "SpawnPoint" that serves as the muzzle.
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) —
// they are written out because they are the record of the intended split, and
// because they are what the lesson hands over verbatim if it is ever taught
// at a lower rung. Do not delete them as "unused".
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'A turret that shoots at me. That is the whole feature, and it is far too big to hold ' +
    'in your head at once — which is exactly why every lesson in this course starts the same way.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Notice the player is close enough',
      does: 'Compare the distance between turret and player against a radius, every frame.',
      doc: 'You already wrote this in 3.1 — Distance() and a range check in Update.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Stamp out a copy of the bullet',
      does: 'Clone the projectile prefab into the scene at the muzzle, at the moment of firing.',
      doc: 'Instantiate(). Met in 2.4 when you spawned pickups; the exact overload is in the quick reference.',
      href: '2.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Tell that copy where to go',
      does: 'Hand the clone a direction, computed once, from the muzzle toward where the player was.',
      doc: 'Vector subtraction, then .normalized — the same pair you used for the diagonal fix in 1.5.',
      href: '1.5.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move it, every frame',
      does: 'Advance the projectile along its stored direction at a speed, without depending on frame rate.',
      doc: 'Your movement method from 2.2, or transform.Translate from 1.4 with deltaTime. The trade-off is yours to make.',
      href: '2.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Get rid of it',
      does: 'Schedule the clone to destroy itself after a few seconds so misses do not accumulate.',
      doc: 'Destroy() — the same call that made a coin vanish in 2.1.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Notice what is NOT on that list: no new API except the spawner, and even that you have ' +
    'seen. Five parts, four of them lifted straight out of lessons you have already finished. ' +
    'That is what a well-sequenced course feels like from the inside — not "here is a new ' +
    'thing you have never seen", but "here is the same five things, pointed at a new target".',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the turret shoot" cannot be looked up. "Clone a prefab at a position" can. ' +
    'If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'There is more than one way to make the bullet travel. Name two, say what each one costs you in components, and say which you picked and why.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. This is the lesson where the
    course stops holding your hand: everything a projectile needs, you have already written
    once, somewhere else. Your job today is to recognise that — and then prove it.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I shoot a projectile?', 4)}

${renderDecompose('3.2', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        No specification this time, and no API table. You have earned the version of this
        lesson where you are handed the <em>behaviour</em> and nothing else. Here is what
        the finished feature does, from the player's seat:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>I walk into the turret's range, and something appears at the <strong>end of the
            barrel</strong> — not at the turret's centre, and not at the world origin.</li>
        <li>It flies toward <strong>where I was standing when it was fired</strong>. If I keep
            moving, it does not follow me — it commits to the shot it took.</li>
        <li>It travels at <strong>the same speed whether I am standing next to the turret or
            at the far edge of its range.</strong></li>
        <li>It <strong>cleans itself up</strong> a few seconds later if it hits nothing.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        Read those four again with your list of parts next to them. Every one of them is a
        part you named, and every one of them is testable by playing the game — which is the
        other half of breaking a problem down. A part you cannot test is not a part yet.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural constraint (the only thing I will hand you):</strong>
        you are editing two files, and they do not know about each other's details.
        <code>TurretController.cs</code> decides <em>when</em> to fire and <em>where</em>
        the bullet starts. <code>Projectile.cs</code> owns everything about the bullet
        after that: which way it goes, how fast, and when it dies. If you find yourself
        writing the bullet's speed into the turret, stop — the turret should not know
        that number exists.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Alone</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Open <code>Assets/Prefabs/Turret_Projectile</code>. You will find an empty
        <code>Projectile</code> component <em>already attached</em> — the same pattern as the
        turret and the coin. Nothing new needs creating. Fill it in, then extend the range
        check you wrote in <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> so it fires.
    </p>

    <p><strong>When you are stuck, this is the order to reread:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> — prefabs, and cloning one at runtime</li>
        <li><a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> — the whole pickup pattern, including the destroy</li>
        <li><a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> — vectors and normalizing, where the diagonal bug was fixed</li>
        <li><a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> — your movement line, and why velocity takes no deltaTime</li>
        <li><a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> — the range check you are extending</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — when you know the name and need the exact spelling</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        That list is the point of the lesson. When you have forgotten how to clone something,
        you do not ask a person and you do not guess — you go and read the lesson where you
        already did it. A working programmer spends most of the day reading, and this list is
        what "reading the documentation for each part" looks like when the documentation is
        your own course.
    </p>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Wire the slots before you press Play.</strong> An unassigned prefab or muzzle
        slot does not complain at startup — it throws at the exact moment you step into range,
        which looks like a firing bug and is really a setup bug. Assign them, <em>then</em> play.
    </div>

    <p>
        Save, wire the slots, and press Play. Walk into range: a projectile appears at the
        muzzle and travels. Do not celebrate the fire rate yet — you are currently firing
        <em>every single frame</em>, because your range check runs every frame. That is exactly
        the problem the next lesson solves.
    </p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>


    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Break a feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own</li>
            <li>Name which part of this feature was lifted from a lesson you had already done</li>
            <li>Say where you would look up a Unity API you have forgotten, without asking a person</li>
            <li>Explain the role of <code>Instantiate</code> in stamping runtime clones</li>
            <li>Explain why a direction vector is normalized, and what goes wrong without it</li>
            <li>Name two ways to move a projectile and state what each one costs</li>
            <li>Explain why your turret currently fires every frame</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — every question this course answers, in the words you would search with</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the projectile lifecycle and aiming in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Instantiate, Destroy and Mathf</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.3.html">3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</a>, where you fix the bullet hose you just built.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.2" title="3.2 Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
